#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083344 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082895 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "54852305-01f3-5036-a31c-d7ddcfd7c100" :question/math-academy-id "e-10717" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{1}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492114 :question-generator/id #uuid "31f57381-8dff-5a7b-89e9-b2ea0e6a407c" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186083344.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5844549f-b897-5a58-bf08-acfbe8efa2a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251276} {:answer/id #uuid "7667e2f4-ee83-5482-af7c-d2bdd84fc202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251526} {:answer/id #uuid "a218e653-d88f-5440-be12-f0ad6810fe89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251949} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cc8e9f-2139-5926-aacc-b95747012288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252321} {:answer/id #uuid "f79ecc31-c3ea-5c7d-ae4d-ea007b99bc9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7667e2f4-ee83-5482-af7c-d2bdd84fc202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251526} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f22f9c0-c715-5c9f-b358-40ff3aa9350f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d988115-5b28-5cc4-948a-8ebc728e32c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118619" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=1+{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+{x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[-{u}^{-1}]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{a}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc22d002-389e-5aff-af28-e8e6f5eb7e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081960} {:answer/id #uuid "30e34f8a-b0af-58be-913e-6d9fb2663d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081961} {:answer/id #uuid "3b073e75-824d-5cb5-b2e0-9f4d981135b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081962} {:answer/id #uuid "6140438b-2a65-5a01-86b7-bd9d34ada694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answ